<header><a href="/">MAE Academy</a><nav aria-label="Primary"><a href="/simulations">Simulations</a> <a href="/research">Research</a> <a href="/library">Library</a> <a href="/about">About</a> <a href="/contact">Contact</a></nav></header>
<main>
<h1>Code Studio</h1>
<article lang="en">
<header><h2>MASM 8086 Mode: Bit Manipulation, Registers and Segments</h2><p>This mode assembles and single-steps real-mode 8086 assembly, so every bit operation is visible as a change in a register, a flag or a memory byte.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and meaning</h3><p>The editor executes MASM source one instruction at a time and shows the result in the Registers and Flags, Execution Trace, Stack and Main Memory tabs. Bit manipulation on the 8086 is done with the logic instructions <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">OR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{OR}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AND</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{AND}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">XOR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{XOR}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">TEST</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{TEST}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SHL</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SHL}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SHR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SHR}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which are the direct hardware counterparts of the C idioms for set, clear, toggle and read: <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">REG</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">|=</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">(1UL</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">&lt;&lt;</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">n)</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{REG |= (1UL &lt;&lt; n)}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is an <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">OR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{OR}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">REG</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">&amp;=</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">~(1UL</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">&lt;&lt;</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">n)</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{REG \&amp;= \textasciitilde(1UL &lt;&lt; n)}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is an <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AND</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{AND}</annotation></semantics></math></span> with the complement mask, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">REG</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">^=</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">(1UL</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">&lt;&lt;</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">n)</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{REG \textasciicircum= (1UL &lt;&lt; n)}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is an <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">XOR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{XOR}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">(REG</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">&gt;&gt;</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">n)</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">&amp;</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">1UL</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{(REG &gt;&gt; n) \&amp; 1UL}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SHR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SHR}</annotation></semantics></math></span> or a <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">TEST</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{TEST}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></section>
<section><h3>Mathematical model</h3>
<h4>Mask algebra on a register</h4><p>A single bit position <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> is selected by the mask <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Setting uses a bitwise OR, clearing uses an AND with the complement <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and toggling uses an XOR, so each operation touches exactly one bit and leaves the other bits of the operand unchanged. For an eight-bit register the valid range is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0 \le n \le 7</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and for a sixteen-bit register it is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0 \le n \le 15</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
<h4>Flags as the observable result</h4><p>After a logic instruction the processor clears <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OF</annotation></semantics></math></span> and updates <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SF</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PF</annotation></semantics></math></span> from the result. <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">TEST</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{TEST}</annotation></semantics></math></span> performs the AND but discards the result, so only the flags change; <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span> when the tested bit is clear. A shift moves the last bit that leaves the register into <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which turns <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SHR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SHR}</annotation></semantics></math></span> into a bit reader.</p>
<h4>Shift counts on the 8086</h4><p>The original 8086 accepts an immediate shift count of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span> only. Any larger count must be placed in <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">CL</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{CL}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, for example <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">MOV</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">CL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">6</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{MOV CL, 6}</annotation></semantics></math></span> followed by <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SHR</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">CL</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SHR AL, CL}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Boundary case: a count of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></span> leaves the operand and the flags untouched.</p>
<h4>Segments, stack and memory</h4><p>Memory is addressed as a segment and an offset, and the physical address is formed by shifting the segment left by four bits and adding the offset, so the space is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>20</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{20}</annotation></semantics></math></span> bytes. The stack grows downward: <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">PUSH</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{PUSH}</annotation></semantics></math></span> decrements <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SP</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SP}</annotation></semantics></math></span> by <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math></span> before storing a word, and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">POP</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{POP}</annotation></semantics></math></span> increments it after loading. Code lives in the code segment, initialised data in the data segment, and the stack in its own segment, which is the 8086 analogue of the .text, .data and stack regions.</p>
<h4>Governing equations</h4>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">∣</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">OR</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">mask</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AL&#x27; = AL \mathbin{|} 2^{n} \qquad (\texttt{OR AL, mask})</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Set a bit with OR and a single-bit mask.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">&amp;</mo><mo>∼</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AND</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">notmask</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AL&#x27; = AL \mathbin{\&amp;} \sim 2^{n} \qquad (\texttt{AND AL, notmask})</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Clear a bit with AND and the complemented mask.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">XOR</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">mask</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AL&#x27; = AL \oplus 2^{n} \qquad (\texttt{XOR AL, mask})</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Toggle a bit with XOR.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">{</mo><mtable rowspacing="0.36em" columnalign="left left" columnspacing="1em"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">&amp;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">TEST</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">mask</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF = \begin{cases} 1 &amp; AL \mathbin{\&amp;} 2^{n} = 0 \\ 0 &amp; \text{otherwise} \end{cases} \qquad (\texttt{TEST AL, mask})</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>TEST sets the zero flag from the masked bit without changing the register.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">&amp;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mtext> after </mtext><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SHR</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">CL</mtext></mrow><mtext> with </mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_n = (AL \gg n) \mathbin{\&amp;} 1, \qquad CF = b_{n-1} \text{ after } \texttt{SHR AL, CL} \text{ with } CL = n</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Read a bit with a right shift and a mask, or through the carry flag.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SEG</mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">OFF</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PA = 16 \cdot \texttt{SEG} + \texttt{OFF}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Physical address from segment and offset (twenty-bit space).</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SP</mtext><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SP</mtext><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">PUSH</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msup><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SP</mtext><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SP</mtext><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">POP</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SP}&#x27; = \texttt{SP} - 2 \;\;(\texttt{PUSH}), \qquad \texttt{SP}&#x27; = \texttt{SP} + 2 \;\;(\texttt{POP})</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Stack pointer behaviour of PUSH and POP for word operands.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section><h3>Canvas parameter and control mapping</h3><dl>
<dt>Language selector (MASM 8086)</dt><dd>Chooses the real-mode 8086 engine. The editor then offers the low-level inspector tabs instead of the variable view.</dd>
<dt>Registers and Flags tab</dt><dd>Shows <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AX</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{AX}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">BX</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{BX}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">CX</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{CX}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">DX</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{DX}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the pointer and segment registers; the HEX, DEC and BIN selector changes the radix so the individual bits <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_n</annotation></semantics></math></span> of the result can be read directly.</dd>
<dt>Flag indicators</dt><dd><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OF</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PF</annotation></semantics></math></span> light up according to the last instruction, giving the result of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">TEST</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{TEST}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and of the bit shifted out by <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SHR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SHR}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
<dt>Run, Step and speed controls</dt><dd>Single-stepping executes one instruction, so the transformation <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AL \to AL&#x27;</annotation></semantics></math></span> of each equation can be checked line by line; breakpoints stop at a chosen instruction.</dd>
<dt>Stack tab</dt><dd>Lists the words pushed at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SS:SP</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SS:SP}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and shows the stack pointer moving by <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math></span> per word.</dd>
<dt>Main Memory tab</dt><dd>Displays bytes at a chosen reference address in hex, decimal or binary, so a memory-mapped style byte can be modified with the same mask idioms.</dd>
<dt>Execution Trace tab</dt><dd>Records each executed instruction with the register changes, which documents the sequence of mask operations.</dd>
</dl></section>
<section><h3>Worked numerical example</h3><h4>Edit the byte <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{AL}</annotation></semantics></math></span> with four mask instructions</h4><p>Start from <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">0A5h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AL = \texttt{0A5h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Set bit <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, clear bit <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, toggle bit <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then test bit <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and read it through the carry flag.</p><ol>
<li><p>Set bit <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the mask is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">08h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{3} = \texttt{08h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">OR</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">08h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{OR AL, 08h}</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">A5h</mtext><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">∣</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">08h</mtext><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>10100101</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">∣</mo><msub><mn>00001000</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>10101101</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">ADh</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{A5h} \mathbin{|} \texttt{08h} = 10100101_2 \mathbin{|} 00001000_2 = 10101101_2 = \texttt{ADh}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Clear bit <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the complement of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">04h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2} = \texttt{04h}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">0FBh</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{0FBh}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AND</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">0FBh</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{AND AL, 0FBh}</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">ADh</mtext><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">&amp;</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">FBh</mtext><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>10101101</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">&amp;</mo><msub><mn>11111011</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>10101001</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">A9h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ADh} \mathbin{\&amp;} \texttt{FBh} = 10101101_2 \mathbin{\&amp;} 11111011_2 = 10101001_2 = \texttt{A9h}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Toggle bit <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the mask is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">80h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{7} = \texttt{80h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">XOR</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">80h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{XOR AL, 80h}</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">A9h</mtext><mo>⊕</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">80h</mtext><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>10101001</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mn>10000000</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>00101001</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">29h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{A9h} \oplus \texttt{80h} = 10101001_2 \oplus 10000000_2 = 00101001_2 = \texttt{29h}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Test bit <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">TEST</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">20h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{TEST AL, 20h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the AND result is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">29h</mtext><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0.22em" rspace="0.22em">&amp;</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">20h</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">20h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{29h} \mathbin{\&amp;} \texttt{20h} = \texttt{20h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which is non-zero, so <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the bit is set.</p></li>
<li><p>Read the same bit through the carry: after <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">MOV</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">CL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">6</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{MOV CL, 6}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">SHR</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AL,</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext mathvariant="monospace">CL</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SHR AL, CL}</annotation></semantics></math></span> the last bit shifted out is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">00h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AL = \texttt{00h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></li>
</ol><p>The byte evolves <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">A5h</mtext><mo>→</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">ADh</mtext><mo>→</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">A9h</mtext><mo>→</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">29h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{A5h} \to \texttt{ADh} \to \texttt{A9h} \to \texttt{29h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the test reports <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the shift leaves <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">00h</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AL = \texttt{00h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></section>
<section><h3>Industrial applications and standards</h3><dl>
<dt>Legacy industrial controllers</dt><dd>Many installed PC-based machine controllers and boot firmware still use real-mode assembly, where output ports are driven by setting and clearing single bits of a byte.</dd>
<dt>Bootloaders and BIOS routines</dt><dd>Early boot code configures hardware through masked read-modify-write sequences on port registers, exactly the <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">OR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{OR}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AND</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{AND}</annotation></semantics></math></span> patterns shown here.</dd>
<dt>Microcontroller education</dt><dd>The register, flag and stack model transfers directly to embedded cores, which makes the 8086 a clear first model of a processor.</dd>
<dt>Reverse engineering and diagnostics</dt><dd>Reading status bytes with <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">TEST</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{TEST}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and decoding flag words is the daily work of firmware debugging and fault analysis.</dd>
</dl></section>
</article>
